# etude-s6z.1 plan — bridge live stages to Claude producers

Track: full STRICT loop. This changes live CLI behavior and passes an
authentication credential to an external producer process.

ANCESTOR: `etude-s6z` (P2) proves four derivation stages can run live through
real LLM producers. This bead supplies the missing producer adapter measured by
a configured plan-stage execution that produces a non-empty LLM artifact.

MEASUREMENT: every stage currently resolves the registry `dev` seat to
`claude -p --model opus` with no prompt argument, while `ExecRunner` discards
stdout and requires `$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`; therefore every configured live stage
fails with `output missing`.

BLAST RADIUS: repository config and runner behavior inside this worktree;
authenticated Claude calls explicitly launched for verification; and
unreferenced blob, tree, and commit objects written to the repository's shared
Git object database for the dirty-tree live proof. No refs are moved by that
temporary object construction. No host packages, system directories,
containers, shared services, or other lane files are changed.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: sending the prompt to Claude can incur a remote request
and charge. The wrapper performs all local validation and prompt construction
before that send. A process or host crash after the send but before atomic
publication can leave a charged request with no stage artifact, and an engine
retry can issue a second request; this residual cannot be eliminated without a
provider idempotency key or durable pre-send protocol that Claude CLI does not
offer. Normal non-crash failures are not retried inside the wrapper.

TOPOLOGY: one `ExecRunner` process invokes one wrapper and one Claude CLI per
stage. `ExecRunner.Run` resolves `WorktreeDir`, assigns it to `cmd.Dir`, and its
existing `TestExecRunner_CWD` regression proves relative commands and agent
lookups execute inside that pinned worktree. There is no new concurrent writer;
the detached worktree remains disposable and the stage artifact is committed
to the run ref only by the existing engine after the wrapper succeeds.

DURABILITY CLASS: at-most-once remote invocation per `ExecRunner.Run` call,
with the crash-window residual described above. The wrapper writes a
scratch-local temporary output, requires it to be non-empty, and renames it to
`$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE` only after Claude exits successfully; retry semantics
remain owned by the existing live engine and gate loop.

COMPLETENESS ENUMERATION: the complete configured stage set is `plan`,
`implement`, `verify`, `docs`, and `review`; each receives an inline command
whose sole stage-specific value is its declared skill/agent name. The complete
wrapper boundary set is missing/invalid agent, missing ETUDE paths, missing
agent definition, zero inputs, ordered multi-input success, Claude failure,
empty or whitespace-only successful Claude output, and successful non-empty
output publication. The exact stage-to-agent set is `plan`→`dev-planner`,
`implement`→`dev-executor`, `verify`→`dev-qa`, `docs`→`dev-docs-writer`, and
`review`→`dev-pr-reviewer`; all five matching files exist under the checked-in
`.claude/agents/`. The workflow migration guard pins those five literal pairs,
asserts every real agent file exists, and covers the two required allowlisted
names.

TRUST BOUNDARY: `.etude/workflow.yaml`, wrapper argv, input role filenames, and
the wrapper's prompt framing are trusted repository/engine-produced control
data. Materialized input contents include prior Claude artifacts and are
untrusted data; the wrapper places each inside explicit begin/end delimiters and
states that delimited content is data, not wrapper instruction. The new Claude
response is likewise untrusted and is published only as the stage artifact.
The OAuth token value is secret process input and must never be printed,
persisted in the prompt, or asserted by value. The wrapper does not opt into
`--dangerously-skip-permissions`; Claude CLI's normal non-interactive permission
mode and the checked-in agent definition govern any tool access.

## Contract decision

A registry seat's `invoke` is a REVIEWER contract: shared prompt on stdin,
structured verdict envelope in `$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`. A workflow stage runner is
a PRODUCER contract: declared inputs plus stage skill in, arbitrary artifact in
`$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`. Reusing `runner: {name: dev}` crosses those contracts and
is the root defect.

Keep `dev` in the registry for schema/default compatibility, but stop using it
as a stage runner. Replace every workflow stage's named runner with an inline
command `bash .etude/dev-stage-runner.sh <stage-skill>`. `bash` is resolved from
PATH before launch; the relative script argument is then resolved inside the
pinned detached worktree. This also passes the skill identity explicitly
without a new reserved environment variable or shell-side YAML parsing.

## Files and implementation

- Add `.etude/dev-stage-runner.sh`:
  - validate one identifier-like agent argument, the three current ETUDE paths,
    and `.claude/agents/<agent>.md` before invoking Claude;
  - collect `ETUDE_INPUTS_DIR/*` in lexical order and fail closed on zero inputs;
  - build a prompt under the engine-owned scratch directory, register cleanup
    before writing it, and enclose complete input contents as role-labelled,
    explicit untrusted-data delimiters;
  - invoke `claude -p --agent <agent>` with the prompt on stdin;
  - write stdout to a temporary sibling, require content beyond ASCII
    whitespace, and rename it to `$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE` only on exit 0; never
    print the environment or token.
- Update `.etude/workflow.yaml` additively and without restructuring stages:
  - preserve `HOME` (required by review/check tooling) and add
    `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to `env_allowlist`;
  - replace all five `runner.name: dev` blocks with the corresponding inline
    wrapper command.
- Update `internal/cli/migration_test.go` so the checked-in workflow guard
  asserts the new producer contract: every stage is inline, every command uses
  the shared wrapper with the stage's exact skill, and the allowlist is exactly
  `HOME` plus `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`. Registry/default tests remain unchanged.
- Add `scripts/dev-stage-runner_test.sh` with a stub `claude`, and add a
  `dev-stage-runner-test` target to `Makefile` plus the aggregate `shell-test`.
- Update `docs/run.md` with the repository-provided wrapper, inline-runner
  rationale, required OAuth allowlist name, and the distinction between stage
  producer output and reviewer verdict envelopes.

